<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canusium</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Canosa di Puglia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canusium, modern Canosa di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Puglia</a>, was one of the main towns of the Daunians in northern Apulia, famed for its rich chamber tombs and painted pottery. It sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the Samnite Wars, and after the Roman defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a> in 216 BCE it sheltered the surviving soldiers. Under Rome it remained a prosperous town on the Via Traiana and later an early bishopric.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canusium.jpg" alt="Canusium" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Habemusluigi Luigi Carlo Capozzi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canosa_panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Canusium, the modern Canosa, lies on the right bank of the Ofanto river, the ancient Aufidus, nearly 20 kilometres from the Adriatic Sea. The town stands on the edge of the Murgia plateau at between 105 and 140 metres above sea level. Its soil is sand or clay over a layer of soft limestone tuff, which is easily excavated. This allowed the digging of underground grottoes and a hypogeum, and the stone taken out of them went into buildings above ground.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> ascribed the foundation of the town to the Homeric hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diomedes">Diomedes</a>. Archaeologists place human presence in the area as far back as the 7th millennium BCE. The surrounding fields were one of the main centres of the Dauni, a northern branch of the Iapyges, and buildings and tombs at Toppicelli point to a wealthy aristocracy. The town became an important commercial centre for craftsmen, above all of ceramics and pottery, and is thought to have become a Greek polis in the age of Magna Grecia.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> defeated the consuls Paullus and Varro at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a> in 216 BCE, Canusium sheltered the fleeing remnants of the Roman army within its walls. In the second year of the Social War it joined the rebels and resisted a Roman siege. It suffered heavily in that conflict or in the civil wars that followed, but became a self-governing municipium in 88 BCE. Some of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> are known from the 4th century, and it came to be called the city of bishops. Bishop Stercorius attended the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Sardica</a> in 343, and the diocese reached its height under Saint Sabinus (514 to 566), later the town's patron saint.</p><p>Earthquakes damaged the town repeatedly, notably in 1361, 1456, 1627, 1659, 1694 and 1851. It was sacked by the Tarantini in 1451 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1803. As a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> it passed through several families, and Tiberio Capece was named prince of Canosa in 1712.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Canosa di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Puglia</a> is a town and comune in the province of Barletta-Andria-Trani in Apulia, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> and Foggia, and is counted among the oldest continually inhabited cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Its Cathedral of San Sabino was founded in the 8th century by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arechis-ii-of-benevento">Arechis II of Benevento</a> and was named after Sabinus in 1101. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-i-of-antioch">Bohemond I of Antioch</a>, a commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, is buried in its mausoleum. The so-called castle is the acropolis of the ancient town, and the remains of a Roman amphitheatre lie at its southern foot. The archaeological museum, founded in 1934, holds about 2,000 finds from the tombs and the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Canosa di Puglia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Daunian society was organised in large settlements such as Arpi, <b>Canusium</b>, Salapia and Sipontum, with chieftains whose wealth appears in their tombs.</blockquote><a href="/state/daunians">Daunians</a></li></ul></section>
